from physics_helpers import q,write_bank
items=[
q(1,'Define electric current.','The rate of flow of electric charge.','4.2.1.2'),
q(1,'What is the SI unit of resistance?','Ohm (Ω).','4.2.1.3'),
q(1,'Which wire in a mains cable is brown?','Live.','4.2.3.2'),
q(1,'What happens to the resistance of an LDR as light intensity increases?','It decreases.','4.2.1.4'),
q(2,'Explain the difference between direct and alternating potential difference.','- Direct pd has **one polarity**.\n- Alternating pd **repeatedly reverses polarity**, causing current to reverse direction.','4.2.3.1','2023 1H Q03.1'),
q(2,'A current of 0.25 A flows for 80 s. Calculate the charge transferred.','**Q = It** = 0.25 × 80 = **20 C**.','4.2.1.2'),
q(2,'A component has 9.0 V across it and carries 0.30 A. Calculate its resistance.','**R = V/I** = 9.0/0.30 = **30 Ω**.','4.2.1.3'),
q(2,'Where should an ammeter and a voltmeter be connected to measure a lamp’s resistance?','- Ammeter **in series** with the lamp.\n- Voltmeter **in parallel across** the lamp.','4.2.1.4','2023 1H Q06.1'),
q(2,'State the potential difference and frequency of the UK mains supply.','About **230 V**, at **50 Hz**.','4.2.3.1'),
q(2,'Two parallel branches carry 0.40 A and 0.65 A from a 12 V supply. State the total current and the pd across each branch.','- Total current = 0.40 + 0.65 = **1.05 A**.\n- Each branch has **12 V** across it.','4.2.2'),
q(2,'Explain why a rod becomes negative when rubbed with a cloth.','- **Electrons transfer onto the rod** from the cloth.\n- It has an excess of negative charge; protons do not transfer.','4.2.5.1'),
q(2,'How does a thermistor’s resistance change when heated, and give one application.','- Its resistance **decreases**.\n- It can be a temperature sensor in a **thermostat**.','4.2.1.4'),
q(3,'A 5 Ω resistor and a 10 Ω resistor are in series across 6.0 V. Calculate the total resistance, the current and the pd across the 10 Ω resistor.','- Rtotal = 5 + 10 = **15 Ω**.\n- I = 6.0/15 = **0.40 A**.\n- V across 10 Ω = 0.40 × 10 = **4.0 V**.','4.2.2'),
q(3,'Explain why a filament lamp’s I–V graph becomes less steep at larger positive potential differences.','- Increasing current **heats the filament**.\n- Ions vibrate more, increasing opposition to electron flow, so **resistance increases**.\n- Current then increases less rapidly with pd; the graph flattens.','4.2.1.4','2023 1H Q06'),
q(3,'An appliance receives 230 V and carries 4.0 A for 60 s. Calculate the energy transferred.','- **P = VI** = 230 × 4.0 = **920 W**.\n- **E = Pt** = 920 × 60.\n- **55,200 J**.','4.2.4.1–4.2.4.2','2022 1H Q03; 2023 1H Q01: electrical-power skills'),
q(3,'Why does adding a resistor in parallel reduce total resistance when the supply pd is fixed?','- It provides an **additional path** for charge.\n- Total current from the supply **increases**.\n- With the same V, **R = V/I decreases**.','4.2.2'),
q(3,'Explain why transmitting a fixed power at high potential difference reduces losses in National Grid cables.','- **P = VI** means higher V gives lower I for the same power.\n- Cable heating power is **I²R**.\n- Lower current therefore reduces unwanted heating and improves transmission efficiency.','4.2.4.3','2023 1H Q01.1: Grid; wider reasoning follows 4.2.4.3'),
q(3,'Explain how an earth wire and fuse protect someone if a live wire touches a metal appliance case.','- The earth wire provides a low-resistance path from the case to earth.\n- A large **fault current** flows.\n- The fuse **melts and breaks the live circuit**, disconnecting the supply.','4.2.3.2'),
q(3,'Explain how a spark can form between a charged object and a nearby conductor.','- The charge creates a **strong electric field** in the gap.\n- The field **ionises air**, allowing it to conduct.\n- Charge flows through the air as a spark, reducing the charge imbalance.','4.2.5.1–4.2.5.2'),
q(4,'A cable has resistance 0.80 Ω and carries 50 A. Calculate its heating power and the heating power if current is halved. State the factor change.','- **P = I²R**.\n- Initial P = 50² × 0.80 = **2,000 W**.\n- New P = 25² × 0.80 = **500 W**.\n- Heating falls to **one quarter**, because current is squared.','4.2.4.1–4.2.4.3'),
q(4,'A component gives these readings: 2.0 V at 0.40 A, and 6.0 V at 0.80 A. Calculate both resistances and explain whether it is ohmic across this range.','- First R = 2.0/0.40 = **5.0 Ω**.\n- Second R = 6.0/0.80 = **7.5 Ω**.\n- Resistance increases, so current is **not directly proportional** to pd.\n- It is **not ohmic** across this range; a heating filament is one possible explanation.','4.2.1.4'),
q(6,'Describe how to investigate the I–V characteristic of a filament lamp. You may use this circuit as a starting point, replacing the test resistor with the lamp.\n\n![Measurement circuit: ammeter in series and voltmeter across the test component.](../Diagrams/resistance-circuit.svg)','- Connect a **low-voltage supply, ammeter and lamp in series**, with a voltmeter across the lamp. Add a variable resistor or use an adjustable supply.\n- Vary pd over a suitable range below the lamp’s rating; record paired **V and I** readings at each setting.\n- Reverse the supply and record negative V and I readings, using the meter correctly.\n- Repeat measurements and investigate anomalies before calculating means. Avoid touching the hot lamp.\n- Plot current vertically against pd horizontally and draw a smooth best-fit curve.\n- The curve is approximately symmetric but becomes less steep as |V| increases because heating increases the filament’s resistance.','4.2.1.4','2023 1H Q06.1'),
q(6,'Plan an investigation of how wire length affects resistance. Include controls and how to process the results.','- Place a uniform wire beside a ruler and use a movable contact to select measured lengths.\n- Connect a low-voltage supply and ammeter in series; a voltmeter measures pd across the selected length.\n- Record **V and I** for several lengths and calculate **R = V/I** each time.\n- Keep material and cross-sectional area fixed. Use low current and switch off between measurements to keep **temperature** approximately constant.\n- Repeat, find mean resistances and plot **R against length**. A straight line through the origin supports direct proportionality.\n- Longer wire provides more interactions opposing electron flow. Check contact quality and avoid hot wire; heating would increase R for a second reason.','4.2.1.3')]
write_bank(2,items,'Electrical power calculations occur in both sampled papers (2022 Q03; 2023 Q01). The 2023 Q06 sequence supplies an I–V practical and lamp-behaviour example; Q03.1 checks dc. Remaining questions broaden coverage of circuit combinations, sensors, safety and electric fields. These are original practice questions rather than copies of the sampled tasks.')
